WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.443
A dairy cow does not pay for herself right away.

2
00:00:03.493 --> 00:00:12.241
Rearing costs come first, and break-even arrives somewhere between the first-and-a-half and the fourth lactation, depending on how much she produces.

3
00:00:12.641 --> 00:00:14.923
So how long do cows actually stay?

4
00:00:14.973 --> 00:00:15.256
In U.

5
00:00:15.306 --> 00:00:15.456
S.

6
00:00:15.506 --> 00:00:18.520
herds, about four point two years from birth.

7
00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:21.252
This paper asks why cows leave, and when.

8
00:00:22.902 --> 00:00:26.348
The data come from the Council on Dairy Cattle Breeding:

9
00:00:26.398 --> 00:00:35.609
five point six million records from forty-two states, for cows born between two thousand five and two thousand ten, and followed for up to seven years.

10
00:00:36.009 --> 00:00:43.506
Herds with fewer than one hundred records were dropped, and so were herds that used five or fewer of the seven culling codes.

11
00:00:43.556 --> 00:00:45.602
Every exit is one of seven reasons.

12
00:00:46.302 --> 00:00:49.290
Survival is estimated with Kaplan-Meier curves.

13
00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:57.644
And because a cow can leave for only one reason, each cause is treated as a competing risk, using the cumulative incidence function.

14
00:00:59.294 --> 00:01:03.373
Overall, thirty-one percent of cows leave the herd each year.

15
00:01:03.423 --> 00:01:06.570
The single largest category is other or injury:

16
00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:08.635
almost a quarter of all exits.

17
00:01:08.685 --> 00:01:16.161
Then reproductive problems, death, poor production, mastitis, sale for dairy purposes, and finally feet and legs.

18
00:01:16.561 --> 00:01:19.033
Group them, and the picture is stark.

19
00:01:19.083 --> 00:01:21.355
Half of all exits are involuntary:

20
00:01:21.405 --> 00:01:24.672
death, mastitis, feet and legs, and reproduction.

21
00:01:24.722 --> 00:01:26.795
A quarter carry no real reason.

22
00:01:26.845 --> 00:01:30.378
Only a quarter are decisions about production or sale.

23
00:01:32.028 --> 00:01:33.796
Timing changes the ranking.

24
00:01:33.846 --> 00:01:36.783
Early in life, sale for dairy purposes leads.

25
00:01:36.833 --> 00:01:40.874
It is the top cause at ages two and three, and then levels off.

26
00:01:41.274 --> 00:01:49.356
Reproductive problems start near zero, then climb steadily after year three, becoming the second-largest cause by year seven.

27
00:01:49.406 --> 00:01:54.003
Other or injury stays on top throughout, and feet and legs stays lowest.

28
00:01:54.703 --> 00:01:59.026
Year by year, the culling rate peaks at age four, at about twenty-four percent.

29
00:02:00.926 --> 00:02:02.285
Who is most at risk?

30
00:02:02.335 --> 00:02:08.054
Open cows are culled at fifty-five percent, against twelve percent for pregnant cows.

31
00:02:08.104 --> 00:02:12.481
At peak lactation, the gap is seventy-six versus thirteen percent.

32
00:02:12.881 --> 00:02:14.772
Risk also rises with parity:

33
00:02:14.822 --> 00:02:20.527
from twenty-two percent in first lactation, to forty-six percent in fourth and later.

34
00:02:20.577 --> 00:02:25.546
Large farms cull the most, at thirty-five percent, medium farms the least.

35
00:02:25.596 --> 00:02:28.959
And Jerseys are most often sold for dairy purposes.

36
00:02:31.059 --> 00:02:39.263
Across birth years from two thousand five to two thousand ten, the curves differ statistically, but by less than four percentage points.

37
00:02:39.313 --> 00:02:40.588
The pattern is stable.

38
00:02:40.988 --> 00:02:41.770
The caveats:

39
00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:47.978
a quarter of exits are labelled only other or injury, and the data end in two thousand seventeen.

40
00:02:50.378 --> 00:02:51.226
The takeaway:

41
00:02:51.276 --> 00:02:53.984
most cows leave reactively, not by choice.

42
00:02:54.034 --> 00:02:59.949
Better recording of culling reasons, and stage-specific care, are where longevity can be won.
